\ Dogfood: Phase 4 slice 6h, migrated to `fill` (P7.S5). The `[Type; Count]`
\ array constructor is deleted; every zero-init probe now uses `fill` with a
\ zero seed, which stores that seed into every slot in a counted loop. Every
\ probe runs *after* a dirtier that fills a nonzero seed into the same stack
\ region and returns, so `fill` reuses stack full of nonzero bytes: a zero
\ read then proves the zero-seed loop ran, not that fresh stack happened to
\ be zero. Without the preamble these are placebos (`Alloc` never zeroes).
\ Each dirtier is sized to the probe's own element width, since an `0 10 fill`
\ dirtier's residue lands on 8-byte-strided offsets only and never overlaps
\ the `0 >i8 10 fill`/`False 4 fill` probes' own byte-granular slots.

import: intrinsics * ;
import: core::prelude * ;
\ A bool is printed with `.` below, and an operator overload does not follow
\ the prelude hub's re-export (one hop only): it comes from the declaring
\ module. `Bool` itself comes through `core::prelude`'s own re-export.
import: core::bool b | . | ;

import: core::combinators c | times | ;

: dirty ( -- ) 99 10 fill drop ;
: dirty_i8 ( -- ) 99 >i8 200 fill drop ;
: dirty_bool ( -- ) True 200 fill drop ;

\ `0 10 fill`: index 9 reads 0 after the dirty preamble.
: probe10 ( -- i64 )
  0 10 fill | a | &a 9 >usize &> @ | v | a drop v ;

\ `0 >i8 10 fill` (size 10 / align 1): index 9 reads 0 and a live neighbour
\ (`guard`, a `0 3 fill` of 5s) stays intact, so a word-granular overrun of the
\ unpadded array would show up as a clobbered neighbour.
: probe_i8 ( -- i8 i64 )
  5 3 fill | guard |
  0 >i8 10 fill | a | &a 9 >usize &> @ | v | a drop
  &guard 0 >usize &> @ | g | guard drop
  v g ;

\ `False 4 fill`: every slot is variant 0 (`False`), not a garbage byte (which
\ prints `True`), so a `False`x4 output can only come from the zero-seed.
: probe_bool ( -- )
  False 4 fill | a |
  &a 0 >usize &> @ .
  &a 1 >usize &> @ .
  &a 2 >usize &> @ .
  &a 3 >usize &> @ .
  a drop ;

\ Terms after the fill's own loop are not dropped (the `terminated`
\ reset), and a `times` after it composes (loop-state hygiene).
: probe_after ( -- )
  0 4 fill | a | a drop
  100 .
  3 ~[ | i | i . ] times
  200 . ;

\ D5: a concrete `0 4 fill` inside a combinator body works for free.
: withbuf inline ( [ -- i64 ] -- i64 )
  | f | 0 4 fill | a | &a 0 >usize &> @ | z | a drop f call z add ;

\ A self-tail-recursive word that constructs an array each iteration: the
\ fill's loop opens *inside* the word's own self-tail loop, so without
\ the save/restore loop-state hygiene the enclosing loop's back-edge is sealed
\ against the wrong header (a compiler panic). `z` is the zero-init index 0.
: recur ( i64 -- )
  | n | 0 4 fill | a | &a 0 >usize &> @ | z | a drop
  n z add .
  n 0 gt ~[ n 1 sub recur ] ~[ ] if ;

: main ( -- )
  dirty probe10 .        \ 0
  dirty_i8 probe_i8 . .  \ 5 0
  dirty_bool probe_bool  \ False False False False
  dirty probe_after      \ 100 0 1 2 200
  [ 5 ] withbuf .        \ 5
  dirty 3 recur ;        \ 3 2 1 0
